Looking at this problem, I need to compute both the scheme-theoretic and set-theoretic images of $\pi: X \to Y$ where $X = \coprod_{n \geq 1} \operatorname{Spec} k[\epsilon_n]/(\epsilon_n^n)$, $Y = \operatorname{Spec} k[x]$, and $x \mapsto \epsilon_n$ on the $n$-th component.

## Proof

**Setup.** On the $n$-th component, the ring map is $\phi_n: k[x] \to k[\epsilon_n]/(\epsilon_n^n)$, $x \mapsto \epsilon_n$. Since $k[x]/(x^n) \cong k[\epsilon_n]/(\epsilon_n^n)$ via $x \mapsto \epsilon_n$, the kernel is $\ker(\phi_n) = (x^n)$.

**Set-theoretic image.** Each $\operatorname{Spec} k[\epsilon_n]/(\epsilon_n^n)$ is a one-point scheme with unique point $(\epsilon_n)$. The image of this point under $\pi$ is the prime $\phi_n^{-1}((\epsilon_n))$. An element $f \in k[x]$ satisfies $\phi_n(f) = f(\epsilon_n) \in (\epsilon_n)$ iff $f(0) = 0$, i.e., $f \in (x)$. So $\phi_n^{-1}((\epsilon_n)) = (x)$ for every $n$. The set-theoretic image is the single point $\{(x)\}$ — the origin of the affine line.

**Scheme-theoretic image.** The scheme-theoretic image is the smallest closed subscheme of $Y$ through which $\pi$ factors. Since $Y = \operatorname{Spec}\, A$ with $A = k[x]$ is affine, a closed subscheme is $V(J) = \operatorname{Spec}(A/J)$ for some ideal $J$, and $\pi$ factors through $V(J)$ iff $J \subseteq \ker(A \to \Gamma(X, \mathcal{O}_X))$.

Now $\Gamma(X, \mathcal{O}_X) = \prod_{n \geq 1} k[\epsilon_n]/(\epsilon_n^n)$, and the map $\phi: k[x] \to \prod_{n \geq 1} k[\epsilon_n]/(\epsilon_n^n)$ sends $x \mapsto (\epsilon_1, \epsilon_2, \epsilon_3, \ldots)$. The kernel is:

$$\ker(\phi) = \bigcap_{n \geq 1} \ker(\phi_n) = \bigcap_{n \geq 1} (x^n).$$

In the PID $k[x]$, we have $(x^a) \cap (x^b) = (x^{\max(a,b)})$. Any nonzero polynomial $f$ has finite order of vanishing $d = \operatorname{ord}_x(f)$ at $x=0$, so $f \in (x^d)$ but $f \notin (x^{d+1})$. Therefore $f \notin \bigcap_{n \geq 1} (x^n)$. This gives:

$$\ker(\phi) = \bigcap_{n \geq 1} (x^n) = (0).$$

The largest ideal $J$ such that $\pi$ factors through $V(J)$ is $J = \ker(\phi) = (0)$. Hence the scheme-theoretic image is:

$$V((0)) = \operatorname{Spec} k[x]/(0) = \operatorname{Spec} k[x] = Y.$$

**Comparison.** The scheme-theoretic image is the **entire affine line** $\operatorname{Spec} k[x]$, while the set-theoretic image is the **single closed point** $\{(x)\}$ at the origin. The scheme-theoretic image is strictly larger.

**Why the discrepancy.** The morphism $\pi$ is **not quasi-compact** (the domain is an infinite disjoint union of non-quasi-compact type). Each component contributes the constraint that the defining ideal lie inside $(x^n)$, and the intersection of all these constraints over $n = 1, 2, 3, \ldots$ collapses to $(0)$. The scheme-theoretic image "remembers" the infinitesimal structure of fat points of every order $n$; since no nonzero polynomial vanishes to all orders simultaneously, no nonzero function is killed, and the scheme-theoretic image is all of $Y$.

By contrast, if we had only finitely many components $n = 1, \ldots, N$, the kernel would be $\bigcap_{n=1}^{N} (x^n) = (x^N)$, giving scheme-theoretic image $\operatorname{Spec} k[x]/(x^N)$ — a fat point supported at the origin, consistent with the set-theoretic image. The phenomenon of the scheme-theoretic image exceeding the set-theoretic image arises specifically from the infinite (non-quasi-compact) nature of the disjoint union.

